PMID- 42403460
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
DCOM- 20260706
LR  - 20260813
IS  - 2666-6677 (Electronic)
IS  - 2666-6677 (Linking)
VI  - 29
DP  - 2026 Oct
TI  - Prevention of atherosclerotic coronary artery disease in tactical athletes.
PG  - 101675
LID - 10.1016/j.ajpc.2026.101675 [doi]
LID - 101675
AB  - Tactical athletes (TAs), including military service members, firefighters, and 
      law-enforcement officers, perform physically demanding occupations in which 
      sudden incapacitation carries substantial individual and public safety 
      consequences. Across TA disciplines, traditional atherosclerotic cardiovascular 
      disease (ASCVD) risk factors (e.g. hypertension, obesity, insulin resistance, 
      sleep disturbance) are highly prevalent and commonly undertreated. Additionally, 
      there are unique occupational stressors (e.g. heat exposure, inhalational 
      hazards, performance enhancing drugs) which may contribute to ASCVD and 
      cardiovascular (CV) mortality. Despite generally high baseline fitness, these 
      populations experience a disproportionate burden of ASCVD, and sudden cardiac 
      death (SCD) is the number one cause of duty-related mortality. Moreover, TAs 
      represent a population in whom baseline fitness can obscure substantial 
      cardiometabolic risk, and because sudden incapacitation has consequences beyond 
      the individual, prevention should shift from symptom-triggered evaluation to 
      systematic risk-factor detection, aggressive risk-factor control, and selective 
      use of non-invasive atherosclerosis imaging. This state-of-the-art review 
      summarizes current evidence on the prevalence of ASCVD risk factors and coronary 
      artery disease (CAD) in TAs, CV screening practices, and management 
      considerations for CAD among TAs.
