DP  - 2026 Aug 24
TI  - Clinical interpretation of cardiac troponin in athletes: From endurance exercise 
      to exertional injury.
LID - 10.1002/pmrj.70203 [doi]
AB  - Exertional heat illness (EHI) and exertional rhabdomyolysis (ER) are increasingly 
      recognized causes of morbidity in competitive and tactical athletes. Cardiac 
      troponin (cTn) elevation, traditionally viewed as a marker of myocardial 
      necrosis, may also be seen in these settings and can create significant 
      diagnostic and prognostic uncertainty. Although transient cTn elevation after 
      endurance exercise is common and generally benign, extrapolation of this paradigm 
      to exertional injury is likely problematic. This narrative review synthesizes 
      current evidence regarding cTn elevation across three related but distinct 
      contexts: isolated endurance exercise, ER, and EHI. We review the current 
      understanding of cTn biology, emphasizing isoform-specific differences between 
      cTn I and T, assay-related considerations, and mechanisms of cTn release beyond 
      irreversible cardiomyocyte necrosis. In isolated endurance exercise, cTn 
      elevation is typically transient, often normalizing over 24-72 hours, and has not 
      been consistently associated with myocardial necrosis or adverse long-term 
      cardiovascular outcomes. In contrast, ER and EHI are different pathophysiologic 
      states characterized by systemic metabolic stress, skeletal muscle injury, and in 
      EHI, systemic hyperthermia and direct thermal injury. The prevalence and 
      prognostic significance of cTn elevation in ER and EHI are poorly defined. 
      Current data, largely extrapolated from nonexertional cohorts, suggest potential 
      associations with increased morbidity and long-term cardiovascular risk. However, 
      prospective studies in ER and EHI are urgently needed to validate these findings. 
      Accordingly, interpretation of cTn in exertional injury requires consideration of 
      clinical context, troponin isoform and kinetics, electrocardiographic findings, 
      and cardiac imaging. Improved understanding of these critical distinctions is 
      essential to guide appropriate evaluation, risk stratification, and safe 
      return-to-activity decisions.
