TI  - Soldiers' Dietary Intake in 3 Settings: In the Field, on Base and at Home.
LID - usag387 [pii]
LID - 10.1093/milmed/usag387 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Adequate nutrition is essential for optimal health, performance and 
      operational readiness of military personnel. Inadequate nutrition has been 
      associated with reduced health and performance, and an increased risk of injuries 
      and dropouts. Despite its importance, limited data are available on the dietary 
      intake of Dutch military personnel. This study aims to assess the energy and 
      nutrient intake of Dutch soldiers in 3 settings: in the field, on base and at 
      home. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Included in the study were 72 soldiers from the 
      Dutch Light Armoured Brigade. Dietary intake during field deployment was assessed 
      using a food intake/waste collection-based method, whereas on base and at home, 
      participants recorded their dietary intake using online food diaries. Dietary 
      intake data were compared against the Military Dietary Reference Intakes (MDRIs). 
      Energy expenditure on base and at home was estimated using online questionnaires 
      and compared against reported energy intake. RESULTS: Fiber intake across all 3 
      settings and carbohydrate intake on base and at home were below the MDRIs. In 
      contrast, protein intake across all settings, as well as carbohydrate intake in 
      the field, met the MDRIs. Intake of total fat, saturated fat and sodium exceeded 
      the MDRIs in all settings. Energy intake was below the MDRIs in all settings, but 
      comparable to energy expenditure on base and at home. CONCLUSIONS: This study 
      shows that the energy intake of the Dutch Light Armoured Brigade was consistently 
      below the MDRI values in the field, on base and at home. To help address this 
      energy deficit, increasing the consumption of fiber-rich carbohydrate sources may 
      be an effective strategy. However, in the field setting, MDRI-based energy 
      expenditure estimates may not have fully reflected the actual activity demands. 
      Therefore, more accurate assessment of energy expenditure and better alignment 
      between ration provision and expected activity demands may help reduce 
      unnecessary carrying load, food waste, and logistical burden, while ensuring 
      sufficient energy availability for military units.
