TI  - Evaluation of Interprofessional Collaboration, Communication, Teamwork, and 
      Prehospital Clinical Competencies Among Military Healthcare Providers During a 
      Medical Trauma Simulation Exercise: A Cross-sectional Study.
PG  - 354-361
LID - 10.1093/milmed/usag097 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Medical Trauma-related injuries necessitate timely and systematic 
      management to minimize long-term complications and mortality. Simulation-based 
      training has become integral in military medicine, providing opportunities for 
      healthcare providers to practice time sensitive and high-stakes skills in a 
      controlled environment.This study aimed to evaluate the impact of an 
      interprofessional medical trauma clinical simulation exercise on the 
      collaboration, communication, teamwork, and prehospital clinical competencies of 
      Canadian Forces Health Services (CFHS) reserve force healthcare personnel. 
      MATERIALS AND METHODS: A cross-sectional, pre-post-test pilot design was 
      employed. Participants engaged in four trauma scenarios, including opioid 
      toxicity, cardiac arrest, airway trauma, and massive hemorrhage. Data were 
      collected using subjective and objective validated tools, focusing on 
      interprofessional education, collaboration, teamwork, satisfaction, and medical 
      trauma prehospital clinical competencies. RESULTS: The study comprised of 21 
      military healthcare providers from medical reserve units. The study found 
      statistically significant improvements in participants' perceptions and attitudes 
      toward interprofessional education and teamwork. Participants also reported high 
      levels of satisfaction and self-confidence in learning. However, competency 
      levels varied across scenarios, with weaker performance in trauma airway 
      management. CONCLUSION: This study demonstrates the multifaceted benefits of 
      interprofessional, simulation-based medical trauma training for military 
      healthcare providers. Although improvements in attitudes and satisfaction were 
      evident, variations in prehospital clinical competency highlight the need for 
      ongoing evaluation and targeted training. This research advances simulation 
      pedagogy within the CFHS and informs future studies to enhance military medical 
      training and operational readiness.
