TI  - From military to civilian nursing: A grounded theory of non-technical competence 
      and the Four-C model.
PG  - 104862
LID - S1471-5953(26)00164-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.nepr.2026.104862 [doi]
AB  - AIM: To explore how British military nurses understand, construct and enact 
      non-technical competence (NTC) across civilian and operational environments and 
      to develop a theory driven model of NTC for military nursing practice. 
      BACKGROUND: Military nurses work in varied and often high-risk settings demanding 
      both technical proficiency and strong NTC. While technical competencies are well 
      defined, NTC remains largely implicit, inconsistently articulated and difficult 
      to assess. This gap limits effective preparation for deployment and operational 
      readiness. DESIGN: A Straussian Grounded Theory design was used to generate a 
      theoretical framework rooted lived experiences of UK Defence Medical Services 
      (DMS) nurses. METHODS: Four focus groups were conducted with twenty-six DMS 
      nurses delivered online or in person. Data were analysed iteratively through 
      open, axial and selective coding supported by constant comparison. Ethical 
      approval was granted by the Ministry of Defence Research Ethics Committee 
      (MODREC). RESULTS: analysis identified three core domains of NTC, conditions, 
      construct, conduct, each shaped by their situational context. Conditions 
      reflected differences between civilian and operational demands. Construct 
      encompassed key components including communication, leadership and emotional 
      intelligence. Conduct related to how NTC is demonstrated, evaluated and refined. 
      Together theses elements generated substantive and formal theories underpinning 
      nursing NTC. CONCLUSIONS: NTC is essential for safe, adaptable and effective 
      military nursing practice yet remains difficult to define and assess. This study 
      presents the first theory-informed model of NTC derived from British military 
      nurses' experiences and introduces the Four-C framework as a tool to enhance NTC 
      development across military and wider healthcare settings.
