TI  - Prevalence of Temporomandibular Disorders in Military, Police, and Firefighter 
      Personnel: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis.
PG  - e1410-e1420
LID - 10.1093/milmed/usaf533 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Military, police, and firefighter personnel routinely face extreme 
      physical and psychological stress, potentially increasing their vulnerability to 
      Temporomandibular Disorders (TMD). While emerging evidence suggests elevated TMD 
      rates in these professions, the collective burden across these services has 
      remained poorly understood, hindering targeted occupational health initiatives. 
      This systematic review and meta-analysis is the first to synthesize global 
      evidence on TMD prevalence in these high-stress uniformed professions. Our 
      primary objectives were to establish a pooled prevalence estimate and investigate 
      sources of heterogeneity to better address this significant occupational health 
      issue. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Adhering to PRISMA/MOOSE guidelines and a 
      registered protocol (PROSPERO: CRD420251025070), we systematically searched 
      databases through April 2025 for observational studies reporting TMD prevalence 
      in adult military, police, or firefighter personnel. Eligible studies were 
      required to use standardized clinical diagnostic criteria (e.g., DC/TMD) or 
      validated symptom questionnaires (e.g., Fonseca Anamnestic Index). Two reviewers 
      independently assessed full-text articles for eligibility and appraised the 
      methodological quality of included studies using the Joanna Briggs Institute 
      (JBI) checklist. A random-effects meta-analysis was performed using R software to 
      derive the pooled prevalence with 95% confidence intervals (CIs). Subgroup 
      analyses were employed to explore sources of heterogeneity. RESULTS: The search 
      yielded 14 unique studies for inclusion, encompassing a total of 4,011 
      individuals. Our random-effects model revealed that the estimated prevalence of 
      TMD across these professions ranges widely, from 37.8% to 55.1% (95% Confidence 
      Interval), with a pooled point estimate of 46.4%. This wide interval is 
      reflective of substantial between-study heterogeneity (I2 = 92.5%; P < 0.0001). 
      Subgroup analyses identified the diagnostic tool used (P = 0.0076) and the 
      continent of study origin (P < 0.0001) as statistically significant sources of 
      this variability. Significant differences were noted by continent, with South 
      American studies reporting the highest rates. No significant difference in TMD 
      prevalence was observed among military, police, and firefighter subgroups 
      (P = 0.3772). CONCLUSIONS: The evidence indicates a substantial health burden 
      from TMD among military, police, and firefighter personnel, establishing it as a 
      significant occupational health concern with nearly one in two individuals 
      potentially affected. Although the considerable heterogeneity across studies 
      underscores the influence of methodological and geographical factors, the overall 
      finding of a widespread problem is robust and contextually plausible given the 
      high-stress nature of these professions. These findings should prompt increased 
      clinical vigilance and inform the development of targeted screening and 
      management strategies to safeguard the well-being and operational readiness of 
      these vital public service populations.
