TI  - How Can French Special Operations Surgical Teams Adapt to the Looming Warfare 
      Challenges?
PG  - e1346-e1349
LID - 10.1093/milmed/usaf500 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The dynamic nature of modern warfare poses significant challenges for 
      Special Operations Surgical Teams (SOST). These teams must remain agile and 
      innovative to deliver life-saving care in austere and evolving combat 
      environments. This report explores practical strategies to enhance the 
      capabilities of French SOST, focusing on modular medical systems, advanced blood 
      management, and the integration of emerging technologies. METHODS: We conducted a 
      comprehensive analysis of current SOST practices and identified key limitations 
      impacting operational efficiency. Proposed innovations were evaluated based on 
      feasibility, scalability, and their potential to address identified gaps. 
      Recommendations were informed by lessons learned from recent French SOST 
      deployments and international military medical research. RESULTS: Key findings 
      include the development of modular surgical kits tailored for diverse operational 
      scenarios, such as extreme climates and prolonged field care. Enhanced blood 
      management systems, including portable refrigeration units and walking blood 
      banks, address logistical challenges in resource-limited settings. The 
      integration of artificial intelligence tools for triage and decision-making, as 
      well as virtual and augmented reality simulations for high-fidelity training, 
      further bolster SOST preparedness and effectiveness. CONCLUSIONS: Innovative 
      strategies, including modularity, advanced blood solutions, and emerging 
      technologies, are essential to ensure French SOST can meet the demands of modern 
      warfare. By addressing current limitations, these advancements will enhance 
      operational readiness, improve patient outcomes, and support interoperability 
      with allied forces. LEVEL OF EVIDENCE: Not applicable (Brief Report).
