TI  - Leader trust behaviours as predictors of functional outcomes in infantry 
      soldiers.
LID - kqag049 [pii]
LID - 10.1093/occmed/kqag049 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Organizational trust has been examined as an expectation that leaders 
      will follow through with promises and act consistently with expectations. Most of 
      this research has involved employee samples in traditional low-risk occupations. 
      AIMS: To examine leader trust behaviours as predictors of multiple indices of 
      functioning, including depression symptoms, symptoms of alcohol misuse, perceived 
      mission readiness and flourishing, in the high-risk occupation of the military. 
      METHODS: Assessed a large cross-sectional sample of infantry soldiers (N = 6384) 
      in garrison who provided responses to a survey assessment. Leader trust 
      behaviours were assessed as the primary predictor. Depression symptoms, alcohol 
      misuse symptoms, perceived mission readiness and flourishing were assessed as 
      outcomes. Ordinary least squares multiple linear regressions were used to examine 
      the relationships between the predictor and outcome variables. RESULTS: The 
      linear regressions revealed that after controlling for covariates, leader trust 
      behaviours predicted fewer depression symptoms (B = -0.16, P < 0.001, 95% 
      CI = -0.19 to -0.13), higher perceived mission readiness (B = 0.39, P < 0.001, 
      95% CI = 0.35 to 0.34) and higher flourishing (B = 0.28, P < 0.001, 95% CI = 0.24 
      to 0.32). Leader trust behaviours did not significantly predict alcohol misuse 
      symptoms. CONCLUSIONS: Leader trust behaviours emerged as a predictor of multiple 
      indices of functioning in the high-risk occupation of infantry soldiers, even 
      after controlling for general leadership and workplace stressors. Leader 
      development focused on the importance of establishing trust in subordinates 
      through specific behaviours could improve outcomes for employees in high-risk 
      occupations.
