DP  - 2025 Nov 21
TI  - Exploring Law Enforcement Officers' Experiences with Athletic Trainers and 
      Work-Related Injury.
LID - 10.3390/ijerph22121769 [doi]
LID - 1769
AB  - Law enforcement is an emerging clinical setting for athletic trainers (ATs), yet 
      little is known regarding law enforcement officers (LEOs) perceptions of the 
      profession. This qualitative study following a general inductive approach 
      explored LEOs' experiences with musculoskeletal injury and their interactions 
      with ATs. Seven officers (N = 7) participated in one-on-one semi-structured 
      interviews. Three themes emerged: (1) roles and responsibilities of ATs, (2) 
      education and training, and (3) impact of injury on LEOs. While participants 
      viewed ATs positively, they demonstrated limited understanding of the 
      profession's scope and expertise. Participants commonly described managing 
      musculoskeletal injuries on their own due to cultural expectations to "push 
      through" pain, limited organizational support, and administrative barriers to 
      care. These patterns reflect broader occupational health and safety concerns 
      within law enforcement and highlight opportunities for ATs to enhance injury 
      prevention and early intervention by promoting a more supportive safety climate 
      and improving access to musculoskeletal care.
