TI  - Army and Marine Aviation Corps Exposome Signatures From the Pharmaceutical 
      Impacts on Tinnitus and Hearing Loss.
PG  - 543-549
LID - 10.1093/milmed/usaf264 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Both tinnitus and hearing loss (THL) have negative impacts on the 
      health and readiness of our active duty military service members (ADSM). A 2019 
      Department of Defense (DoD) report to Congress included medical record data 
      (2005-2015) indicating that active duty career helicopter pilots had a 12.45 
      incidence rate of hearing loss per 1,000 person-years. The report also informs on 
      the burden of injuries and recovery times to mission readiness. Our current study 
      also assesses potential links between rotary wing pilot occupation and acute and 
      chronic medical conditions. Here, we examined the rates of hearing loss and 
      tinnitus following the original investigation period, including exposure to 
      antibiotic treatment as an additional risk factor. MATERIALS AND METHODS: A 
      retrospective study was conducted using de-identified data spanning January 2013 
      to December 2022. Data were sourced from the Medical Assessment and Readiness 
      System (MARS) at Womack Army Medical Center. Healthcare encounters, including 
      Army and Marine Corps Aviation Community (AMAC) that involved antibiotic use 
      before THL diagnosis were analyzed to capture corresponding health, 
      sociodemographic, and occupational data. Multinomial mixed-effects logistic 
      regression was used to assess the relationship between predictor variables, 
      diagnoses of THL (e.g., ICD9: 389, ICD10: H93.19), and antibiotic classes 
      prescribed. RESULTS: Our AMAC population (N = 98,835) included THL diagnoses from 
      2013 to 2022. Over 10 years, helicopter pilots in our sample had almost 3 times 
      the rate of tinnitus diagnosis and twice the rate of hearing loss when compared 
      to ground crew controls. Antibiotic prescriptions for ADSM with diagnoses of 
      hearing loss were also elevated. The rate of antibiotic prescription preceding 
      diagnosis was doubled in AMAC service members with tinnitus. When adjusted for 
      age, sex, race, body mass index, tobacco use, and alcohol use, the data showed 
      that each prescription increased the odds of tinnitus diagnosis by 13.1% (95% CI, 
      11.3-14.9) and hearing loss diagnosis by 9.0% (95% CI, 7.2-10.8). CONCLUSIONS: 
      Our results identified tetracycline and macrolide class antibiotic use as a risk 
      factor for THL within the AMAC. These tetracycline and macrolide class antibiotic 
      prescriptions accounted for 90% of the prescriptions in our cohort. Our analyses 
      also showed that aminoglycosides, known for causing hearing loss, were not a 
      relevant class of prescription drugs associated with THL. Our approach combines 
      big data science analytics from MARS in ADSMs suffering from THL after exposures 
      to certain classes of antibiotics. Results from our analytics can inform 
      objective biochemical indicator species observations. Our primary goal is to 
      operationalize ADSMs' unique physiological markers with corresponding 
      sociodemographic and occupational data to generate prospective personalized 
      algorithms. These can be used as actionable predictive models to optimize 
      ADSM-specific health and military readiness.
