TI  - Who Takes Care of Doc: Care-Seeking Challenges of US Navy Enlisted Support 
      Staff-Corpsmen.
PG  - e2087-e2093
LID - 10.1093/milmed/usaf134 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Navy corpsmen (medics) serving in Marine Corps units face unique 
      barriers in seeking personal medical care because of their dual roles as 
      caregivers and service members. The challenges of care quality, privacy, and 
      self-care expectations in military contexts are amplified by operational demands 
      and the close-knit nature of military communities, influencing corpsmen's health 
      care-seeking behaviors and overall readiness. This study aims to identify the 
      specific barriers and facilitators affecting treatment-seeking behaviors among 
      Navy corpsmen in the III Marine Expeditionary Force, Okinawa, Japan, to inform 
      targeted interventions that support corpsmen's health and enhance operational 
      effectiveness. MATERIALS AND METHODS: We conducted a secondary analysis of 
      transcripts from semi-structured interviews and focus groups originally collected 
      for an educational needs assessment as part of a curriculum reform project for 
      Navy corpsmen. The dataset included 47 participants, including 31 health care 
      workers, who provided insights into their experiences within the III Marine 
      Expeditionary Force in Okinawa, Japan. Themes were inductively coded using 
      Dedoose 9.2 with specific attention to challenges related to care-seeking 
      behavior, such as complexities of receiving care within military units, issues of 
      privacy, and stigma associated with seeking help from peers. RESULTS: Variability 
      in care quality emerged as a barrier, as inconsistent experiences eroded trust in 
      the health care system. Privacy concerns, stemming from overlapping personal and 
      professional environments, increased reluctance to seek care, especially for 
      sensitive issues. The expectation of self-reliance further discouraged corpsmen 
      from accessing formal health care services. However, professionalism among 
      corpsmen acted as a facilitator, promoting trust and encouraging care-seeking 
      behaviors even when treatment involved peers. CONCLUSION: Addressing these 
      barriers requires consistent training to ensure care quality, measures to improve 
      confidentiality, and educational efforts to challenge self-reliance norms. 
      Reinforcing professionalism can promote a supportive medical environment, 
      enhancing corpsmen's health and operational readiness.
