TI  - US Navy Sailors Modify Their Eating Behaviors to Pass Cyclic Physical Readiness 
      Tests.
PG  - 611-621
LID - S1499-4046(24)00349-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jneb.2024.05.232 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Investigate the influence of physical readiness tests (PRTs) on eating 
      behaviors among US Navy Sailors. DESIGN: Focused ethnography using interviews and 
      military policies. SETTING: Three US Naval installations. PARTICIPANTS: 
      Active-duty enlisted Sailors (n = 32). PHENOMENON OF INTEREST: Eating behaviors. 
      ANALYSIS: Authors analyzed data iteratively with data collection using domain, 
      taxonomy, and thematic analysis to identify culturally relevant codes, domains, 
      and themes. RESULTS: Five themes corresponded with PRT timing and Sailors' 
      perceived ability to meet standards: (1) maintain usual eating habits, (2) get 
      ready and switch it up, (3) make weight-damage control, (4) return to 
      baseline-PRT is over, and (5) eat whatever-PRT is canceled. In contrast to the 
      PRT policy's goal for Sailors to maintain standards and a healthy lifestyle, many 
      Sailors modified their usual eating behaviors to pass the test. CONCLUSION AND 
      IMPLICATIONS: Changes in eating behaviors during PRT cycles highlight a culture 
      of getting ready vs staying ready, suggesting many Sailors do not eat a 
      nutrient-dense diet to maintain the minimum physical fitness and body composition 
      standards. There is a need for nutrition education for healthy weight 
      maintenance, weight gain prevention, and healthy weight loss among military 
      personnel.
