TI  - Short report: impact of traveler's diarrhea on United States troops deployed to 
      Thailand.
PG  - 699-701
AB  - Among a United States military unit of 170 personnel deployed to Utapao, Thailand 
      for a three-week training exercise, 40% experienced diarrheal disease, and 12% 
      sought medical treatment for diarrhea. Most illness clustered within the first 
      two weeks of arrival and individuals were ill an average of 3.6 days. Fifty-five 
      percent of cases lost two days of work and 15% required treatment with 
      intravenous fluids. Bacterial pathogens were recovered from 38% of 16 stools 
      submitted, with Campylobacter jejuni the most common. Four (12.5%) of 32 persons 
      who voluntarily submitted paired sera exhibited a four-fold increase in IgG 
      antibody titer to C. jejuni. Traveler's diarrhea continues to be an important 
      problem with a serious potential to impact the mission readiness of even small 
      military units deployed overseas.
