TI  - ECG Quiz: A Murmur in an Asymptomatic Athlete.
PG  - 135-7
LID - 10.3810/psm.1997.08.1548 [doi]
AB  - A 35-year-old, active duty Army officer presented for a screening physical exam 
      before entering special training for HALO (High Altitude Low Opening) parachute 
      qualification. This training involves jumping from aircraft at extreme altitudes 
      with deployment of the parachute at relatively low altitudes (free fall). At the 
      time of this evaluation he was assigned to a special operations airborne unit on 
      active jump status. Careful review of his recent exercise history revealed a 
      gradual decrease in endurance over the past several years, which he felt was due 
      to decreased training intensity. His exercise consisted of running 3 to 4 miles 
      four times a week and physical fitness training with his military unit three 
      times a week. He continued to achieve high scores on the Army Physical Readiness 
      Test.
