TI  - Epidemiology of enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli-associated diarrheal disease 
      occurring on board U.S. Navy ships visiting Asian ports.
PG  - 475-8
AB  - Diarrhea represents a major health threat to U.S. military forces overseas, 
      especially in developing countries. For military units, this illness can 
      adversely affect combat readiness. USNAMRU-2 investigators joined several U.S. 
      Navy ships to assess the epidemiology of diarrhea illness as a result of port 
      visits to Asia. The primary goals were to enumerate episodes of diarrhea 
      associated with port visits, identify epidemiologic factors leading to illness, 
      and characterize the etiologic agents. Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (ETEC) 
      was the most common organism isolated from patients presenting with diarrhea and 
      represented 22% of diarrhea cases. Vomiting and abdominal pain differentiated 
      ETEC from other causes of diarrhea.
